 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The concerned inverse problem is to recover the anisotropic conductivity A from suitable internal observation of the solution u. ... Ammari et al. [3] studied the recovery of an anisotropic conductivity proportional to a known conductivity tensor with data acquired from diffusion tensor imaging.\n\n# Imaging anisotropic conductivity from internal measurements with mixed least-squares deep neural networks☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Siyu Cen ^{a}, Bangti Jin ^{b}, Xiyao Li ^{c}, Zhi Zhou ^{a}\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.jcp.2024.113648\n\n\n## Highlights\n\n  * •\n\nDevelop a deep learning based algorithm for recovering anisotropic conductivity.\n\n  * •\n\nProvide mathematical guarantees of the reconstructions.\n\n  * •\n\nShowcase the algorithm with extensive 2D and 3D numerical experiments.\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this work we develop a novel algorithm, termed as mixed least-squares deep neural network (MLS-DNN), to recover an anisotropic conductivity tensor from the internal measurements of the solutions. It is based on applying the least-squares formulation to the mixed form of the elliptic problem, and approximating the internal flux and conductivity tensor simultaneously using deep neural networks. We provide error bounds on the approximations obtained via both population and empirical losses. The analysis relies on the canonical source condition, approximation theory of deep neural networks and statistical learning theory. We also present multiple numerical experiments to illustrate the performance of the method, and conduct a comparative study with the standard Galerkin finite element method and physics informed neural network. The results indicate that the method can accurately recover the anisotropic conductivity in both two- and three-dimensional cases, up to 10% noise in the data.\n\n## Keywords\n\nDeep neural network\n\n;\n\nAnisotropic conductivity\n\n;\n\nMixed least-squares\n\n;\n\nError estimate\n\n## Introduction\n\nThe conductivity value of an object varies significantly with the composition and type of materials, and its accurate imaging can provide valuable structural information about the object. This fact underpins several important medical imaging modalities, e.g., electrical impedance tomography (EIT), current density impedance imaging, and acousto-electrical tomography. It is widely accepted that the conductivity values of most biological tissues, e.g., cell membranes, muscle fibers and nerve fiber structures, must be investigated using an anisotropic tensor model. See the works [40], [9], [2] for comprehensive overviews on mathematical models and relevant theoretical results for related inverse problems in bio-engineering. In this work, we aim at identifying an anisotropic conductivity tensor in elliptic problems from internal data using deep neural networks (DNNs). Let be a simply connected open bounded domain with a smooth boundary ∂Ω. Consider the following boundary value problem for the function u with a Neumann boundary condition\\{\\begin{array}{cccc}\\hfill -\\mathrm{\\nabla }\\cdot (A\\mathrm{\\nabla }u)& =f,\\hfill & \\hfill \\phantom{\\rule{1em}{0ex}}& \\text{in }\\mathrm{\\Omega },\\hfill \\\\ \\hfill \\overrightarrow{n}\\cdot (A\\mathrm{\\nabla }u)& =g,\\hfill & \\hfill \\phantom{\\rule{1em}{0ex}}& \\text{on }\\partial \\mathrm{\\Omega },\\hfill \\end{array} where \\overrightarrow{n} denotes the unit outward normal direction to the boundary ∂Ω. The functions and in (1.1) are the given source and flux, respectively, and are assumed to satisfy the standard compatibility condition in order to ensure solvability. In addition to the Neumann setting, we also consider the Dirichlet setting of the elliptic problem, i.e., . Let {\\mathcal{S}}_{d} denote the set of all symmetric matrices equipped with the inner product , and define the set with . The anisotropic conductivity A is assumed to belong to the following admissible set Then the conductivity tensor is uniformly elliptic. This and standard elliptic regularity theory ensure that problem (1.1) has a unique solution with the normalization condition [35, Lemma 2.1]. We use the notation to indicate the dependence of the solution u to problem (1.1) on .\n\nThe concerned inverse problem is to recover the anisotropic conductivity A from suitable internal observation of the solution u. This class of inverse problems has been extensively studied in both mathematics and bio-engineering communities. Given one current density magnitude, the reconstruction problem in a known conformal class was studied in [23]. In a series of interesting works, Bal et al. investigated the uniqueness of the reconstruction of an anisotropic resistivity from current densities (i.e., A∇u) [5], [6] and power densities (i.e., for solutions of problem (1.1)) [37], [38], [39]. For example, Bal et al. [6] established a minimum number of current densities that is sufficient to guarantee a unique local reconstruction of the conductivity tensor A. Ammari et al. [3] studied the recovery of an anisotropic conductivity proportional to a known conductivity tensor with data acquired from diffusion tensor imaging. Theoretically, Hsiao and Sprekels [25] derived the stability estimate for the reconstruction of matrix-valued coefficients of the form ; Rannacher and Vexler [46] analyzed the reconstruction error of matrix-valued coefficients (with a finite number of unknown parameters) in case of finitely many point-wise observations. Kohn and Lowe [33] extended the mixed formulation to the anisotropic case by introducing variational forms for the least-squares objectives, resulting in a convex functional in the anisotropic conductivity A and the flux . However, no numerical experiments were presented for the reconstruction of A. Inspired by the work [33], we approximate both current flux σ and conductivity A separately using two DNNs, adopt a least-squares objective for all the equality constraints, and minimize the overall population or empirical loss with respect to the DNN parameters. The use of DNNs in place of the Galerkin FEM effectively exploits the inductive bias and expressivity of DNNs for function approximations, which can be highly beneficial for numerical recovery. By leveraging the existing approximation theory of DNNs [19] and tools from statistical learning theory [4], [8], we derive rigorous error bounds on the approximations in terms of the accuracy of the noisy observational data {z}^{\\delta }, DNN architecture (depth, width and parameter bound), and the numbers of sampling points in the domain Ω and on the boundary ∂Ω, etc. This is carried out for both population and empirical losses (resulting from the Monte Carlo quadrature of the integrals). These error bounds provide solid theoretical underpinnings for the approach. In practice, the proposed approach is easy to implement, robust against data noise, and capable of handling inverse problems in three-dimensional spaces. To the best of our knowledge, previous works have not numerically validated the reconstruction of anisotropic conductivities in 3D. These distinct features make the proposed method highly attractive, and the numerical results clearly show its significant potential. The systematic development of the DNN formulations, rigorous error analysis and extensive numerical validation represent the main contributions of this work.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReconstruction of coefficients in scalar second-order elliptic equations from knowledge of their solutions | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/audio/1111.5051)\nciteturn999search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Guillaume Bal, Gunther Uhlmann ... The research establishes a mathematical framework for the unique and stable reconstruction of complex-valued and anisotropic coefficients in scalar second-order elliptic equations from internal measurements of their solutions. ... The research establishes a mathematical framework for the unique and stable reconstruction of complex-valued and anisotropic coefficients in scalar second-order elliptic equations from internal measurements of their solutions.\n\n# Reconstruction of coefficients in scalar second-order elliptic equations from knowledge of their solutions\n\nReconstruction of coefficients in scalar second-order elliptic equations from knowledge of their solutions\n\nNovember 21, 2011\n\nGuillaume Bal, Gunther Uhlmann\n\n### Summary\n\nThe research establishes a mathematical framework for the unique and stable reconstruction of complex-valued and anisotropic coefficients in scalar second-order elliptic equations from internal measurements of their solutions. This approach demonstrates significantly improved stability and enables the reconstruction of coefficient equivalence classes, and even individual coefficients under specific conditions, providing a theoretical basis for quantitative hybrid medical imaging modalities.\n\nThe research establishes a mathematical framework for the unique and stable reconstruction of complex-valued and anisotropic coefficients in scalar second-order elliptic equations from internal measurements of their solutions. This approach demonstrates significantly improved stability and enables the reconstruction of coefficient equivalence classes, and even individual coefficients under specific conditions, providing a theoretical basis for quantitative hybrid medical imaging modalities.\n\n  * Traditional inverse problems, such as the Calderón problem, often face inherent non-uniqueness issues due to gauge transformations and poor, typically logarithmic, stability when reconstructing medium properties solely from boundary measurements.\n  * Reconstructing general complex-valued and anisotropic coefficients from boundary-only data is particularly challenging, limiting the detail and accuracy of material characterization.\n  * Many existing models in biomedical imaging techniques (e.g., QPAT, TE, MRE) rely on simplified assumptions like isotropic or real-valued coefficients, which do not fully capture the complexity of biological tissues.\n\n  * The authors formulate the problem to reconstruct coefficients (a, b, c) of a scalar second-order elliptic equation by leveraging internal measurements of multiple solutions (u_j) throughout the domain, rather than just boundary data.\n  * A local reconstruction procedure transforms the original equation to obtain gauge-invariant coefficients (α, β) from ratios of solutions, enabling their explicit and stable determination using derivatives of the internal data.\n  * Strategies are developed for global reconstruction using the Runge Approximation Property and unique continuation, and methods are proposed to uniquely determine the gauge function (and thus individual coefficients) under specific conditions, such as a vanishing convection term.\n\n  * Unique and stable reconstruction of the equivalence class of coefficients (a, b+∇·a, c) is proven using a sufficient number of internal solutions, with significantly improved stability estimates in W^(m,∞) norms compared to logarithmic stability for boundary-only methods.\n  * In several practical scenarios, a minimum of *I_n = 1/2 n(n+3)* internal solutions is shown to be sufficient for unique and global reconstruction, particularly for coefficients near constant/isotropic forms, or for general anisotropic tensors in two dimensions.\n  * If the convection term *b* is identically zero, the gauge transformation function τ can be uniquely and stably determined, enabling the full reconstruction of individual coefficients (a, b, c) rather than just their equivalence class.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3rd European Congress of Mathematics (https://www.math.uni-bielefeld.de/~rehmann/ECM/cdrom/3ecm/pdfs/pant2/2e/07520.pdf)\nciteturn999s